 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Time:** The method involves computing the PDF and CDF ($O(N_c)$), generating random numbers ($O(N_f)$), binary search ($O(N_f \log N_c)$), and interpolation ($O(N_f)$) [G]. Overall complexity is dominated by $O(N_f \log N_c)$, where $N_c$ is the number of coarse samples and $N_f$ is the number of fine samples [G].
-*   **Memory:** Requires storage for the CDF and intermediate tensors, proportional to $O(N_c + N_f)$ [G].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   Performance depends on the hardware and resolution [G]. On a single 2080 Ti, training a NeRF on a low-res scene (e.g., `fern`) takes approximately 8 hours for 200k iterations [S7, unofficial].
-*   The method allows for fewer ray points while maintaining accuracy, reducing the computational load compared to uniform sampling [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Scaling:**
-*   The method scales linearly with the number of rays and samples [G].
-*   It is suitable for large-scale volume rendering due to its efficiency in concentrating samples in high-density regions [G].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Differentiable, allowing gradients to back-propagate through the sampling process [S1, unofficial]. Numerically stable with proper epsilon handling [S1, S7, unofficial]. Efficient importance sampling [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires coarse samples and weights as input [S4, unofficial]. Performance may degrade if coarse weights are inaccurate [S1, unofficial].
-
-**When to Use:**
-*   Use when importance sampling is needed to reduce variance in volume rendering [S1, unofficial].
-*   Use when differentiable sampling is required for end-to-end optimization [S1, unofficial].
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-*   Avoid when computational resources are extremely limited and coarse weights are not available [S4, unofficial].
-*   Avoid when the scene has very low density variations, making importance sampling less beneficial [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \log M)$ per ray, where $N$ is the number of fine samples and $M$ is the number of coarse bins, dominated by the `searchsorted` operation [S4, S7, unofficial]. The PDF/CDF computation is $O(M)$.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(M)$ per ray to store the CDF and intermediate tensors [S7, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** In NeRF, using 64 coarse and 128 fine samples with hierarchical sampling significantly reduces PSNR degradation compared to uniform sampling with the same total sample count, effectively doubling the quality for the same computational budget [S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Concentrates samples in high-density regions, improving reconstruction quality of thin structures and surfaces [S1, S3, unofficial]. Differentiable, allowing gradient flow to the coarse network [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a coarse pass first, adding latency [S4, unofficial]. Performance depends on the quality of the coarse weights; poor coarse estimates lead to poor fine sampling [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When computational resources are limited but high-quality reconstruction of complex geometry is needed [S3, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance is critical and a single pass is required, or when the scene is uniformly dense [G].
 
 ## Background
-Hierarchical Sampling (Inverse CDF Sampling) originates from the NeRF (Neural Radiance Fields) paper by Mildenhall et al. [S2, S5, unofficial]. It was introduced to improve the efficiency of volume rendering by concentrating samples in regions of high density [S8, unofficial]. The method has been widely adopted in subsequent works, including [S1, S4, S7, unofficial].
+Hierarchical Sampling was introduced in the seminal NeRF paper "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" by Mildenhall et al. (2020) [S2, S9, unofficial]. It was developed to address the inefficiency of uniform sampling in neural radiance fields, where most samples fall in empty space. The method has since become a standard component in many NeRF variants and volume rendering pipelines [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. It is widely used in academic research and open-source implementations such as the official NeRF repository and various "NeRF from Scratch" tutorials [S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
 
